{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/13/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9344f635\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.\n- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.\n\nRule:\n- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.\n- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.\n- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.\n\nKey insight:\n- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a25697e4\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nDock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.\n- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.\n\nRule:\n- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.\n- Rotate/flip the tool so one color fills the cavity exactly; the other stays outside.\n- If the docking orientation uses a reflection, swap the tool's two colors.\n\nKey insight: Cavity size determines tool–frame pairing; reflection triggers color swap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"25e02866\" lines=\"14-31\">\n<description>\nMerge same-sized filled rectangular objects by overlaying their internal marker pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background surrounds two or more separated 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Every object is a filled rectangle of the same size.\n- Each rectangle has the same majority base color plus one or more non-base marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Create one output rectangle with the shared object size, filled with the shared base color.\n- For each input rectangle, copy every non-base marker pixel to the same relative location\n  in the output.\n- Marker overlays must either land on base color or agree in color with an existing marker.\n\nKey insight:\nThe separated rectangles are layers of the same template; their colored marker pixels\ncombine by local coordinates while the repeated base fill is just the output canvas.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a395ee82\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nStamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).\n- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).\n- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.\n\nRule:\n- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.\n- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.\n- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.\n- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n\nKey insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the \"self\" copy, which is uniquely recolored.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f931b4a8\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nCount the two upper quadrant control regions to size an output made from the lower quadrant pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an even square split into four equal quadrants.\n- Black is the background.\n- The upper-left and upper-right quadrants are control regions.\n- The lower-left quadrant is a color source tile; the lower-right quadrant is either a stencil tile or empty.\n\nRule:\n- Output height is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-left quadrant.\n- Output width is the number of non-background pixels in the upper-right quadrant.\n- If the lower-right stencil has foreground pixels, tile it over the output; each stencil background pixel is filled from the lower-left quadrant cell at the corresponding coarse tile position.\n- If the lower-right stencil is empty, tile the lower-left quadrant directly over the output.\n\nKey insight:\n- The top quadrants specify only dimensions, while the bottom quadrants specify a two-level repeated pattern: a fine stencil and coarse fill colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"14b8e18c\" lines=\"40-57\">\n<description>\nMark every complete square object with red pixels just outside its corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform background contains one or more monochrome 4-connected foreground objects.\n- The relevant objects are square frames or filled squares: their bbox is square\n  and all cells on the bbox border have the object's color.\n- Non-square bars and incomplete fragments may also appear and are ignored.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For each complete square object, paint red on the two outside background\n  pixels orthogonally adjacent to each of the square's four corners.\n\nKey insight: The transformation is not based on size or color; it selects\nobjects whose bounding box is a complete square border and labels only their\nexposed outer corner-adjacent cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5d588b4d\" lines=\"10-28\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left horizontal bar into a wrapped sequence of increasing and decreasing foreground runs.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- All foreground pixels form one contiguous horizontal bar in the top row.\n- The bar starts at the left edge; its length is the control value n.\n\nRule:\n- Build a one-dimensional sequence of foreground runs with lengths\n  1, 2, ..., n, n-1, ..., 2, 1.\n- Separate adjacent runs by one background pixel.\n- Wrap the sequence row-major across the input width and pad the final row\n  with background pixels.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output preserves the input width; the bar length controls the run-length\ndiamond, and the apparent 2D pattern is just that 1D sequence wrapped into rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"50c07299\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nMove the diagonal foreground segment to the next longer run along the same up-right diagonal.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color. The foreground pixels form a\ncontiguous one-pixel-wide segment on a single anti-diagonal.\n\nRule\nErase the current segment. If its columns are c0..c1 and its length is L, draw the same\ncolor on columns c1+1..c1+L+1 along the same anti-diagonal.\n\nKey insight\nThe segment advances past its current upper-right end by one whole segment position, and\nthe next segment is one pixel longer than the current one.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4e7e0eb9\" lines=\"31-49\">\n<description>\nTransform each 9x9 panel by mirroring across its center guide line or filling all four block slots from its lone exemplar.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is made of 9x9 panels separated by one-cell-wide grid lines. Each\npanel has four monochrome 3x3 slots at the quadrant positions, with either a\nfull non-background center row, a full non-background center column, or no\nguide line.\n\nRule\nA panel with a full center column is reflected left-to-right, and a panel\nwith a full center row is reflected top-to-bottom. A panel with no guide line\nhas exactly one slot color that appears once and one slot color that appears\nthree times; recolor all four slots to the once-occurring slot color.\n\nKey insight\nThe center guide line encodes the mirror axis, while panels without a guide\nline are completed by copying the structurally unique slot color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6bcdb01e\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nExtend the edge-touching green start bar through the corridor, turning at each teal blocker until the path leaves the grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: a 2-pixel 4-connected start bar and many blocker pixels.\n- Exactly one pixel of the start bar lies on the grid border.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the border pixel of the bar and move through the other bar pixel into the grid.\n- Paint every traversed non-blocker pixel with the start color.\n- When the next cell is a blocker, turn to the unique perpendicular non-blocker neighbor; when the next cell is outside the grid, stop.\n\nKey insight: The blocker pixels define a one-cell-wide corridor, so every interior collision has exactly one valid 90-degree turn.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e9fc42f2\" lines=\"26-43\">\n<description>\nAssemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground\nobjects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are\nboundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a\nmatched docking pair.\n\nRule\nCrop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two\noccurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve\neach object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled\nforeground on the original background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and\nthe marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nScale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background color.\n- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).\n\nRule:\n- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
